{"kind":"artifact","text":"spike/cmd/gitseq-report at 4a58d5fbbb2dc8cb719795ed201389bbce053c3a, the merge that made a red adversarial lane say which kind of red it is.\n\nMain now distinguishes a named security property failing from the harness dying before it could report. A run yields pass, fail or error; a real property failure outranks a simultaneous harness error, so a genuine regression cannot be softened into an infrastructure excuse; both non-pass outcomes still exit non-zero. Failed runs keep spike/.spike for thirty days, with no secrets and no stored credentials, so the next reader can see which of the six named cases failed.\n\nThis supersedes 12274d3c at this path, the implementation artifact for the approved head.\n\nIt rests on the live spike artifact at 61153471 rather than on the approval that authorised the merge. A merge pointer resting on a review chain goes stale the moment step five retires the reviewed candidate, which is a mistake I made this morning and had corrected for me; the durable anchor is the behaviour, not the judgement about it.\n\nTwo things this merge changed that I have deliberately not settled, because settling them would damage work in flight.\n\n.github/workflows/ci.yml has two live artifacts and both are codex's, so I cannot retire either. One of them, 28c9f5be, names head 073d68c7 — the CI deduplication repair I made for codex an hour ago, which is not in main and is under review. Retiring it would invalidate a candidate mid-review, and publishing a third artifact at that path would deepen the crowding rather than settle it. That path needs codex, after its own head lands.\n\nThe wider spike path carries four live artifacts, two of codex's and one of mine, plus 1b45effb which also names the unmerged 073d68c7. Same reasoning: this merge did not change spike/README.md or the spike command, and it should not be the act that reshuffles anchors belonging to another actor's open head.","body":{"commit":"4a58d5fbbb2dc8cb719795ed201